{
  "requirement": "Habeas MUST parse Rust, Python, TypeScript, CSS, Markdown, TOML, and YAML\nsources with pinned tree-sitter grammars, selected by file extension compared\ncase-insensitively. When validating outline evidence inputs, Habeas MUST report\nthe line of the first syntax error in a recovered outline. Every\nother text file MUST use a plain-text profile in which each line that is not\nblank is an anonymous outline node of kind `line`. Each supported language MUST\nhave an outline profile that maps grammar node kinds to short outline kind names\nand, for each mapped kind, states whether a node is named, and by which fields\nor children, or anonymous. Only mapped kinds MUST appear in the outline, except\nthat a Markdown profile MAY additionally recognize colon-fenced directive\ncontainers under {cite}`requirements.sdk.sources.markdown.fence_precedence`\nand expose them as outline nodes under\n{cite}`requirements.sdk.sources.markdown.fenced_blocks`. Such containers MUST\npreserve source ranges and containment, and MUST NOT be recognized within\nliteral code or raw HTML. Other nodes MUST be transparent, so their outline\ndescendants appear under the nearest outline ancestor. A profile MAY attach grammar siblings to an outline\nnode, such as Rust attributes and doc comments preceding an item, so the outline\ntree need not match the grammar tree exactly; attached siblings are part of that\nnode for its line range and hash. Every file's outline MUST have a root node\nfor the whole file, independent of language. Source in no other outline node,\nsuch as top-level statements no profile maps, MUST belong to the root node;\nprofiles MUST NOT invent fallback kinds for it. Comments MUST NOT be outline nodes. Profiles MUST be\ndocumented. Upgrading a grammar or profile MAY change outlines and MUST be\ntreated as a deliberate change.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "I judge the tests adequate for `requirements.references.outlines`. I did not request implementation source, because the supplied tests were enough to map each obligation to a scenario and an assertion.\n\n- **Grammar selection by extension, case-insensitive.** `profiles_are_selected_by_extension_case_insensitively` asserts the profile for upper- and mixed-case extensions. It also checks that a file with no extension, or with a trailing `.txt` such as `notes.rs.txt`, gets the plain-text profile. Each language also has its own profile test, run with real sources:\n  - Rust: `rust_selectors_follow_syntactic_ancestry`\n  - Python: `python_profile_outlines_defs_classes_and_statements`\n  - TypeScript and TSX: `typescript_profile_outlines_declarations`\n  - CSS: `css_profile_names_rules_by_normalized_selectors`\n  - Markdown: `markdown_profile_nests_sections_by_heading`\n  - TOML and YAML: `toml_and_yaml_profiles_name_keys`\n- **Pinned grammars.** `grammar_dependencies_are_pinned_to_exact_versions` checks that all eight tree-sitter dependencies in `Cargo.toml` use exact `=x.y.z` pins. That also covers the \"upgrade is a deliberate change\" clause.\n- **Line of the first syntax error.** `reports_recovered_syntax_errors_with_lines` checks the reported line for a Rust error. It also checks that a TOML source with several errors reports the earliest one, and that the message begins with the line prefix. The evidence-validation path is covered in `outline_inputs_survive_moves_and_unhashed_comments_but_not_edits` (`tests/evidence_boundaries.rs`), which expects a `cannot parse rust source: line N:` message for a broken file.\n- **Plain-text profile.** `plain_text_lines_are_interchangeable_anonymous_nodes` checks that blank lines are skipped and the remaining lines are anonymous `line@` nodes. `outline_lists_nested_nodes_without_text_or_inputs` checks that `notes.txt` has kind `line` and a null name.\n- **Profile mapping and naming rules.**\n  - `markdown_views_respect_profile_field_and_child_naming_rules` covers named-by-field and named-by-first-child, both when the field or child is present and when it is missing.\n  - `profile_rules_map_only_matching_contexts_and_keep_other_nodes_transparent` covers \"only mapped kinds appear\", \"other nodes are transparent\" (descendants attach to the nearest outline ancestor), and the resolve error messages.\n  - `item_macros_are_anonymous_nodes_and_expression_macros_are_not` covers anonymous kinds.\n- **Markdown directive containers.**\n  - `paragraphs_and_nested_directives_have_independent_source_ranges` covers source ranges and containment.\n  - `unfinished_directives_retain_their_body_and_leave_the_outer_closer_outside` and `nested_fence_lengths_and_closer_whitespace_preserve_the_right_containers` cover nesting, closers and unclosed fences.\n  - `literal_code_inline_code_and_html_shield_directive_looking_text` covers \"MUST NOT be recognized within literal code or raw HTML\". It uses backtick and tilde fences, inline code, `<div>` and comment HTML.\n- **Attached siblings.** `identity_hashes_include_own_attached_siblings_but_not_descendants` and `hashes_skip_whitespace_and_plain_comments_but_not_doc_comments` check that doc comments and attributes belong to their node's range and hash. They also show that plain comments do not.\n- **Root node and no fallback kinds.**\n  - `file_level_inner_doc_comments_and_unmapped_code_belong_to_the_root` and `python_profile_outlines_defs_classes_and_statements` check that unmapped top-level code, front matter and inner docs belong to the root.\n  - `no_profile_invents_fallback_kinds` checks every profile for `stmt`, `block`, `node` or `item` fallbacks. It also checks that unmapped-only files have just the root node.\n- **Comments not outline nodes.** The CSS, TOML, YAML and Markdown profile tests include comments, and none of them appears in the expected node lists. The Markdown test additionally checks that comments are held in `outline.comments`.\n- **Documented profiles.** `every_profile_mapping_is_documented` checks `md/reference.md` against every profile's extensions and kind rules, and checks the plain-text row.\n- **Redaction.** `redaction_never_changes_an_outline` is a related check that outlines are built from the original text.\n\nEvery clause has at least one test that exercises the behaviour and asserts its outcome. I found no marking gaps and no acknowledged gaps. The cited fence requirements have their own tests, which I did not reassess.",
  "references": "f7fc9b17499048056681dfeb94ce231d25712b3e115e0e8f391e100eac152fd9",
